/** @mainpage %Qore openldap Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section openldapintro Introduction

    The openldap module exposes functionality from the <a href="http://www.openldap.org">openldap</a> library as a Qore API, allowing qore programs to communicate with LDAP servers.

    This module is released under the <a href="http://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/lgpl-2.1.html">LGPL 2.1</a> and is tagged as such in the module's header (meaning it can be loaded unconditionally regardless of how the Qore library was initialized).

    Like all Qore components, the openldap module is thread-safe.  The @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient class represents a single network connection to the LDAP server and therefore wraps requests in a mutual-exclusion lock to ensure atomicity and thread-safety.

    Asynchronous APIs are used internally to enforce time limits for each LDAP operation.  The default timeout for all LDAP operations is set in the @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::constructor() "LdapClient::constructor()" method with the \c "timeout" option, however each method requiring communication with the LDAP server also takes an optional timeout argument that allows the default timeout to be overridden for specific calls.  If no \c "timeout" option is specifically set in the @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::constructor() "LdapClient::constructor()", the default timeout for new objects is automatically set to 60 seconds.

    <b>Overview of Operations Supported by the LdapClient Class</b>
    |!Operation|!Method|!Description
    |search|@ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::search() "LdapClient::search()"|Search for entries and attributes; supports paged results via \c "page-size" option
    |add|@ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::add() "LdapClient::add()"|Add entries to the Directory Information Tree
    |modify|@ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::modify() "LdapClient::modify()"|Modify existing entries
    |delete|@ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::del() "LdapClient::del()"|Delete existing Entries
    |compare|@ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::compare() "LdapClient::compare()"|Compare attribute values
    |rename|@ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::rename() "LdapClient::rename()"|Rename or move entries to another location in the Directory Information Tree
    |change password|@ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::passwd() "LdapClient::passwd()"|Changes the LDAP password for the given user
    |SASL bind|@ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::saslBind() "LdapClient::saslBind()"|SASL bind with any mechanism (GSSAPI, DIGEST-MD5, EXTERNAL, etc.)
    |extended operation|@ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::extendedOp() "LdapClient::extendedOp()"|Perform generic LDAP extended operations (Who Am I?, Cancel, etc.)

    The underlying %LDAP functionality is provided by the <a href="http://www.openldap.org">openldap library</a>.

    @section openldap_installation Installation notes

    If you intend to use the <i>oracle</i> module along with <i>openldap</i> on Linux, then you will probably be facing the issue https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/1043.
    Currently we don't have a generic solution for the issue, but there is a work-around using \c LDPRELOAD.

    @section openldap_helper_modules Helper Modules

    The openldap module includes additional helper modules for common use cases:

    @subsection openldap_connection_pool Connection Pooling

    The \c LdapConnectionPool module provides efficient connection pooling for multi-threaded applications:

    @code{.qore}
%new-style
%requires LdapConnectionPool

# Create a connection pool
LdapConnectionPool pool("ldap://localhost:389", {
    "binddn": "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com",
    "password": "admin",
}, {
    "min_connections": 2,
    "max_connections": 10,
    "idle_timeout": 300s,
});

# Use the pool with automatic connection management
pool.withConnection(sub(LdapClient ldap) {
    hash<auto> result = ldap.search({
        "base": "dc=example,dc=com",
        "filter": "(objectClass=*)"
    });
});

# Or get/release connections manually
LdapClient ldap = pool.get();
# ... use connection ...
pool.release(ldap);
    @endcode

    @subsection openldap_helper_classes Helper Classes

    The \c LdapHelper module provides convenient classes for working with LDAP data:

    - \c LdapEntry - Object-oriented wrapper for LDAP entries
    - \c LdapSearchIterator - Iterator for efficient result processing
    - \c LdapDn - RFC 4514-compliant Distinguished Name parser with escaping support
    - \c LdapClientHelper - Extended client with convenience methods (including \c whoAmI())
    - \c LdapUrl - RFC 4516 LDAP URL parser
    - \c LdapSchema - LDAP schema introspection (objectClasses, attributeTypes)

    @code{.qore}
%new-style
%requires LdapHelper

LdapClientHelper helper("ldap://localhost:389", {
    "binddn": "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com",
    "password": "admin",
});

# Check if connection is working
if (helper.ping()) {
    printf("Connection OK\n");
}

# Get a single entry
*LdapEntry entry = helper.getEntry("uid=testuser,ou=people,dc=example,dc=com");
if (entry) {
    printf("User: %s\n", entry.getAttributeString("cn"));
    printf("Email: %s\n", entry.getAttributeString("mail"));

    if (entry.hasObjectClass("posixAccount")) {
        printf("UID Number: %s\n", entry.getAttributeString("uidNumber"));
    }
}

# Iterate over search results
LdapSearchIterator iter = helper.searchIterator({
    "base": "ou=people,dc=example,dc=com",
    "filter": "(objectClass=inetOrgPerson)",
});
while (iter.next()) {
    LdapEntry e = iter.getValue();
    printf("Found: %s\n", e.dn);
}
    @endcode

    @subsection openldap_async Asynchronous Operations

    The \c LdapAsync module provides non-blocking LDAP operations using the Qore Future/Promise pattern:

    @code{.qore}
%modern
%requires LdapAsync

LdapAsyncClient async("ldap://localhost:389", {
    "binddn": "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com",
    "password": "admin",
});

# fire off parallel searches
LdapFuture f1 = async.searchAsync({"base": "ou=people,dc=example,dc=com", "filter": "(uid=user1)"});
LdapFuture f2 = async.searchAsync({"base": "ou=groups,dc=example,dc=com", "filter": "(cn=admins)"});

# collect results (blocks until each completes)
hash<auto> users = f1.get(30s);
hash<auto> groups = f2.get(30s);
    @endcode

    @subsection openldap_ldif LDIF Import/Export

    The \c LdifHelper module provides LDIF (RFC 2849) reading and writing:

    @code{.qore}
%modern
%requires LdifHelper

# Read entries from an LDIF file
LdifReader reader("/path/to/data.ldif");
while (*hash<LdifEntryInfo> entry = reader.readEntry()) {
    printf("DN: %s\n", entry.dn);
}

# Write entries to an LDIF file
LdifWriter writer("/path/to/output.ldif");
writer.writeEntry("dc=example,dc=com", {"objectClass": ("top", "domain"), "dc": "example"});
    @endcode

    @subsection openldap_ad Active Directory Compatibility

    The \c LdapActiveDirectory module provides Active Directory-specific features:

    @code{.qore}
%modern
%requires LdapActiveDirectory

ActiveDirectoryClient ad("ldap://dc.example.com:389", {
    "binddn": "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com",
    "password": "admin",
});

# Automatic paged search (handles AD's 1000-entry limit)
hash<auto> results = ad.searchPaged({"base": "dc=example,dc=com", "filter": "(objectClass=user)"});

# Range retrieval for large groups
list<auto> members = ad.getRangedAttribute("cn=LargeGroup,dc=example,dc=com", "member");

# Decode binary Active Directory attributes
string guid = ActiveDirectoryClient::decodeObjectGUID(binary_guid_value);
string sid = ActiveDirectoryClient::decodeObjectSid(binary_sid_value);
    @endcode

    @section openldap_examples Examples

    The bulk of the LDAP functionality provided by this module is encapsulated in the @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient class.  There are also four example programs included with the openldap module: qldapsearch, qldapmodify, qldapdelete, qldapadd, and qldappasswd.  These are somewhat similar in usage to the standard LDAP commands, however are designed to provide user-friendly examples of command-line Qore-based LDAP integration, and, for example, do not support or work with LDIF formatted data, etc (for example, qldapsearch outputs the results of a search as a multi-line formatted Qore hash).

    @par Performing an LDAP Search
    @code
%new-style
%requires openldap
string uri = "ldap://localhost";
hash<auto> conn_opts = {
    "binddn": "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com",
    "password": "password",
    "timeout": 20s,
    "starttls": True,
};
LdapClient ldap(uri, conn_opts);
hash<auto> search = (
    "base": "ou=people,dc=example,dc=com",
    "filter": "(uid=username)",
    "attributes": ("uidNumber", "gidNumber"),
    "scope": LDAP_SCOPE_BASE,
);
hash result = ldap.search(search);
    @endcode
    @see @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::search() "LdapClient::search()"

    @par Adding an LDAP Entry
    @code
%new-style
%requires openldap
string uri = "ldap://localhost";
hash<auto> conn_opts = {
    "binddn": "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com",
    "password": "password",
    "timeout": 20s,
    "starttls": True,
};
LdapClient ldap(uri, conn_opts);
ldap.add("uid=test,ou=people,dc=example,dc=com", {"objectclass": "inetorgperson", "sn": "Test", "cn": "User Test"});
    @endcode
    @see @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::add() "LdapClient::add()"

    @par Modifying Attributes of an Existing LDAP Entry
    @code
%new-style
%requires openldap
string uri = "ldap://localhost";
hash<auto> conn_opts = {
    "binddn": "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com",
    "password": "password",
    "timeout": 20s,
    "starttls": True,
};
LdapClient ldap(uri, conn_opts);
ldap.modify("uid=test,ou=people,dc=example,dc=com", {"mod": LDAP_MOD_REPLACE, "attr": "gidnumber", "value": 1000});
    @endcode
    @see @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::modify() "LdapClient::modify()"

    @par Deleting an Existing LDAP Entry
    @code
%requires openldap
string uri = "ldap://localhost";
hash<auto> conn_opts = {
    "binddn": "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com",
    "password": "password",
    "timeout": 20s,
    "starttls": True,
};
LdapClient ldap(uri, conn_opts);
ldap.del("uid=test,ou=people,dc=example,dc=com");
    @endcode
    @see @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::del() "LdapClient::del()"

    @par Comparing Attribute Values
    @code
%new-style
%requires openldap
string uri = "ldap://localhost";
hash<auto> conn_opts = {
    "binddn": "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com",
    "password": "password",
};
LdapClient ldap(uri, conn_opts);
bool match = ldap.compare("uid=test,ou=people,dc=example,dc=com", "uid", "test");
printf("Match: %y\n", match);
    @endcode
    @see @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::compare() "LdapClient::compare()"

    @par Renaming an LDAP Entry
    @code
%new-style
%requires openldap
string uri = "ldap://localhost";
hash<auto> conn_opts = {
    "binddn": "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com",
    "password": "password",
};
LdapClient ldap(uri, conn_opts);
ldap.rename("uid=oldname,ou=people,dc=example,dc=com", "uid=newname", "ou=people,dc=example,dc=com");
    @endcode
    @see @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::rename() "LdapClient::rename()"

    @par Changing a User's Password
    @code
%new-style
%requires openldap
string uri = "ldap://localhost";
hash<auto> conn_opts = {
    "binddn": "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com",
    "password": "password",
};
LdapClient ldap(uri, conn_opts);
ldap.passwd("uid=test,ou=people,dc=example,dc=com", "oldpassword", "newpassword");
    @endcode
    @see @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::passwd() "LdapClient::passwd()"

    @par SASL Bind (GSSAPI, DIGEST-MD5, EXTERNAL, etc.)
    @code
%modern
%requires openldap
LdapClient ldap("ldap://localhost");
ldap.saslBind({
    "mechanism": "DIGEST-MD5",
    "authcid": "username",
    "password": "secret",
    "realm": "EXAMPLE.COM",
});
    @endcode
    @see @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::saslBind() "LdapClient::saslBind()"

    @par Paged Search (for directories with size limits)
    @code
%modern
%requires openldap
LdapClient ldap("ldap://localhost", {"binddn": "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com", "password": "admin"});
# The page-size option automatically handles RFC 2696 paged results
hash<auto> all_entries = ldap.search({
    "base": "dc=example,dc=com",
    "filter": "(objectClass=person)",
    "page-size": 500,
});
    @endcode

    @par Using Server Controls
    @code
%modern
%requires openldap
LdapClient ldap("ldap://localhost", {"binddn": "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com", "password": "admin"});
# Pass server controls with any operation
hash<auto> result = ldap.search({
    "base": "dc=example,dc=com",
    "filter": "(objectClass=*)",
}, NOTHING, ({
    "oid": LDAP_CONTROL_MANAGEDSAIT,
    "critical": True,
},));
    @endcode

    @par Extended Operations (Who Am I?)
    @code
%modern
%requires openldap
LdapClient ldap("ldap://localhost", {"binddn": "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com", "password": "admin"});
hash<auto> result = ldap.extendedOp(LDAP_EXOP_WHO_AM_I);
if (exists result.value) {
    printf("Bound as: %s\n", result.value.toString("UTF-8"));
}
    @endcode
    @see @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::extendedOp() "LdapClient::extendedOp()"

    @par Binary Attribute Handling
    @code
%modern
%requires openldap
# Known binary attributes (jpegPhoto, userCertificate, etc.) are automatically
# returned as binary type. Additional binary attributes can be specified:
LdapClient ldap("ldap://localhost", {
    "binddn": "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com",
    "password": "admin",
    "binary-attributes": ("customBinaryAttr",),
});
# Per-search override:
hash<auto> result = ldap.search({
    "base": "dc=example,dc=com",
    "filter": "(uid=test)",
    "binary-attributes": ("thumbnailPhoto",),
});
    @endcode

    @par LDAP Transactions (RFC 5805)
    @code
%modern
%requires openldap
%requires LdapHelper

LdapClient ldap("ldap://localhost", {"binddn": "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com", "password": "admin"});

# RAII transaction — auto-aborts if exception is thrown before commit
{
    LdapTransaction txn(ldap);
    list<hash<auto>> ctrls = txn.getControls();

    # all operations are atomic
    ldap.add("uid=user1,ou=people,dc=example,dc=com", {
        "objectClass": "inetOrgPerson", "sn": "User1", "cn": "User 1",
    }, NOTHING, ctrls);
    ldap.add("uid=user2,ou=people,dc=example,dc=com", {
        "objectClass": "inetOrgPerson", "sn": "User2", "cn": "User 2",
    }, NOTHING, ctrls);

    txn.commit();  # atomic: both succeed or both roll back
}
    @endcode
    @note The server must support RFC 5805. Check rootDSE \c supportedExtension for OID 1.3.6.1.1.21.1.
    @see @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::txnStart() "LdapClient::txnStart()"
    @see @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::txnEnd() "LdapClient::txnEnd()"

    @section openldap_limitations Limitations

    This module currently has no known functional limitations. All major LDAP operations, controls,
    SASL authentication, paged results, extended operations, transactions, and asynchronous operations
    are supported.

    @note LDAP transaction support (RFC 5805) requires server-side support; not all LDAP servers
    implement this feature.

    @section openldap_release_notes Release Notes

    @subsection openldap_rel20 openldap Module 2.0

    <b>New Features:</b>
    - added LDAP transaction support (RFC 5805) via @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::txnStart()
      "txnStart()" / @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::txnEnd() "txnEnd()" with RAII \c LdapTransaction class
    - added server and client controls support on all LDAP operations; see @ref ldap_control_constants
    - added SASL authentication with any mechanism (GSSAPI, DIGEST-MD5, EXTERNAL, SCRAM, etc.) via
      @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::saslBind() "LdapClient::saslBind()"
    - added paged results (RFC 2696) via the \c "page-size" search option; handles Active Directory's
      1000-entry limit transparently
    - added generic extended operations via @ref OpenLdap::LdapClient::extendedOp() "LdapClient::extendedOp()";
      see @ref ldap_extop_constants for common OIDs
    - added binary attribute support with built-in known-binary attribute list and opt-in via
      \c "binary-attributes" and \c "all-binary" options
    - added RFC 4514-compliant DN parsing with escaped character support, multi-valued RDNs,
      and \c escape() / \c unescape() static methods
    - added \c LdapAsync module with \c LdapFuture and \c LdapAsyncClient for non-blocking parallel
      LDAP operations using the Qore Future/Promise pattern
    - added \c LdifHelper module with \c LdifReader and \c LdifWriter for LDIF (RFC 2849) import/export
    - added \c LdapActiveDirectory module with \c ActiveDirectoryClient for AD-specific features:
      auto-paged search, range retrieval, objectGUID/objectSid decoding, deleted object search
    - added \c LdapSchema class for LDAP schema introspection (objectClasses, attributeTypes)
    - added \c LdapUrl class for RFC 4516 LDAP URL parsing
    - added \c whoAmI() convenience method to \c LdapClientHelper
    - added connection pool health checks, automatic reconnection, and \c withConnectionRetry() with
      configurable retry policy
    - added 14 LDAP control OID constants and 5 extended operation OID constants

    <b>Bug Fixes:</b>
    - <b>critical fix</b>: fixed \c copy() constructor not re-binding to the LDAP server because the
      bind parameters hash was never saved after a successful bind
    - updated copyright to 2026

